<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Android has blocked cleartext HTTP by default since API 28, and the Jetson
    serves its MJPEG video over plain HTTP on the local network. Without this
    file the live view fails to connect with no useful error.

    The Jetson's address is discovered at runtime through Firestore, so it
    cannot be named here: a domain-config entry takes a hostname or a literal
    IP, and there is no CIDR form to permit the private ranges as a group.
    Permitting cleartext generally is therefore the only workable option.

    What this does not weaken: Firestore and Cloud Messaging are reached through
    https URLs, so they keep certificate validation and stay encrypted. The only
    cleartext traffic this app generates is the video stream, and it never
    leaves the house network.

    Known limitation worth stating in the thesis: anyone already on the same
    WLAN can read that video stream. Closing it properly means terminating TLS
    on the Jetson with a certificate the phone trusts, which is out of scope for
    the prototype but is the correct fix for a deployed installation.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
